WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.553 --> 00:00:11.327
This is am-i.exposed: a free, open-source privacy scanner for Bitcoin.

2
00:00:11.487 --> 00:00:16.024
It shows you what a chain analyst can see about your transaction or your address.

3
00:00:16.187 --> 00:00:18.644
The whole analysis runs in your browser.

4
00:00:18.644 --> 00:00:23.439
Only blockchain data is fetched, from mempool.space or from your own node.

5
00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:29.014
This notice reminds you that, on the open network, mempool.space sees your IP.

6
00:00:29.014 --> 00:00:31.684
For more privacy, use Tor or a VPN.

7
00:00:35.487 --> 00:00:39.526
In the search box you can paste an address, a transaction ID,

8
00:00:39.526 --> 00:00:44.081
an xpub or a descriptor, and even a PSBT before you broadcast it.

9
00:00:44.253 --> 00:00:47.905
Below are real examples, with grades from A plus down to F.

10
00:00:48.087 --> 00:00:49.750
And here are more examples:

11
00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:53.881
CoinJoins, a dust attack, Satoshi's address or a fresh address.

12
00:00:54.053 --> 00:00:58.586
Scroll down, and the analyst lens compares two views of a real transaction.

13
00:00:58.753 --> 00:01:01.108
The wallet view shows inputs and outputs.

14
00:01:01.108 --> 00:01:05.838
The analyst view marks the likely payment, the likely change, and what leaked.

15
00:01:09.753 --> 00:01:12.342
Paste a transaction ID and press Scan.

16
00:01:12.453 --> 00:01:14.407
While it scans, you see each stage:

17
00:01:14.407 --> 00:01:18.340
fetching the transaction, then tracing its inputs and its outputs.

18
00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:22.882
Then come the 34 checks. Green raises the score, red lowers it.

19
00:01:23.053 --> 00:01:28.417
Here is the verdict: an F, 24 out of 100. Critical privacy failures.

20
00:01:28.587 --> 00:01:32.403
Twelve issues. Nine are already on-chain and cannot be undone;

21
00:01:32.403 --> 00:01:34.439
three are habits you can fix.

22
00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:37.465
The top recommendation gets straight to the point:

23
00:01:37.465 --> 00:01:40.367
your wallet sends change back to the input address.

24
00:01:40.367 --> 00:01:43.120
Use one that creates fresh change addresses.

25
00:01:43.387 --> 00:01:45.535
This bar takes you to each section:

26
00:01:45.535 --> 00:01:49.284
overview, transaction, findings, score and analyst tools.

27
00:01:49.453 --> 00:01:52.076
The flow shows one input and two outputs.

28
00:01:52.076 --> 00:01:56.675
One goes back to the very same input address: the change is exposed.

29
00:01:56.853 --> 00:02:00.651
Linkability colours the flow by Boltzmann link probability.

30
00:02:00.651 --> 00:02:03.154
Here, both links are deterministic.

31
00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:05.309
The analyst view adds its reading:

32
00:02:05.309 --> 00:02:08.601
this output goes back to an address the sender owns.

33
00:02:08.753 --> 00:02:11.994
In the evidence, leaks come first. This one is critical:

34
00:02:11.994 --> 00:02:16.816
the same address on the input and the output reveals the change with certainty.

35
00:02:16.987 --> 00:02:18.976
Each finding shows its confidence,

36
00:02:18.976 --> 00:02:22.136
who can exploit it and whether it is already on-chain,

37
00:02:22.136 --> 00:02:24.200
plus a concrete recommendation.

38
00:02:24.353 --> 00:02:27.392
Below are the minor signals and the privacy strengths.

39
00:02:27.553 --> 00:02:29.575
The breakdown explains the score:

40
00:02:29.575 --> 00:02:33.746
it starts from a base of 70, and each finding adds or subtracts.

41
00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:36.826
And the exposure table shows who can see each leak:

42
00:02:36.826 --> 00:02:40.380
the public, companies that know your identity, or a state.

43
00:02:44.587 --> 00:02:48.488
For contrast, scan a Whirlpool CoinJoin made with Ashigaru.

44
00:02:48.353 --> 00:02:52.986
Five inputs and five identical outputs of 2,500,000 satoshis.

45
00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:01.624
Result: A plus, 100 out of 100. The CoinJoin is recognized and scores points.

46
00:03:01.787 --> 00:03:04.148
The Link Probability Matrix is computed in

47
00:03:04.148 --> 00:03:06.759
your browser with the Boltzmann algorithm.

48
00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:11.932
1,496 valid interpretations and 10.55 bits of entropy.

49
00:03:11.932 --> 00:03:17.657
Any input could own any output, with no deterministic link.

50
00:03:21.753 --> 00:03:23.307
Back to the F transaction.

51
00:03:23.307 --> 00:03:27.622
Under Analyst tools, the graph lets you follow the coins hop by hop.

52
00:03:27.987 --> 00:03:33.115
Press the plus to expand a hop. This input comes from Huobi, a known exchange.

53
00:03:33.287 --> 00:03:37.379
Full screen gives you more tools: the heat map, fingerprints and links.

54
00:03:37.553 --> 00:03:40.856
The heat map colours each transaction by its privacy score.

55
00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:45.083
And the taint flow shows how these funds reach Binance four hops later.

56
00:03:48.887 --> 00:03:52.181
Before you send bitcoin, paste the destination address.

57
00:03:52.153 --> 00:03:57.166
This one has never been used: low risk. Sending here creates no address reuse.

58
00:03:57.320 --> 00:04:02.727
By contrast, a heavily reused address, like Satoshi's, gets an F with zero points.

59
00:04:02.887 --> 00:04:07.946
Its destination risk is critical: it has received funds tens of thousands of times.

60
00:04:07.946 --> 00:04:10.756
Sending there would link your transaction.

61
00:04:14.853 --> 00:04:19.589
From any result you can save it to local bookmarks, or share a link or a report.

62
00:04:20.053 --> 00:04:24.367
In the settings you choose the network: mainnet, testnet4 or signet.

63
00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:27.300
Under Advanced you can use your own mempool instance,

64
00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:29.700
so your queries never leave your network.

65
00:04:29.853 --> 00:04:32.897
Open the site in Tor Browser and it is detected,

66
00:04:32.897 --> 00:04:36.508
and queries go through mempool.space's onion address.

67
00:04:36.687 --> 00:04:39.265
And if you run an Umbrel or StartOS node,

68
00:04:39.265 --> 00:04:42.849
install am-i.exposed from its app store in one click.

69
00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:45.121
The guide collects privacy techniques,

70
00:04:45.121 --> 00:04:48.080
a wallet comparison and the most common mistakes.

71
00:04:49.187 --> 00:04:50.904
And it works on your phone, too.

72
00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:55.002
The chain is public, and someone is always watching.

73
00:04:55.002 --> 00:04:57.144
Scan it before someone else does.

74
00:04:59.020 --> 00:05:02.517
am-i.exposed: free, open source, and no tracking.
